Query 5: WHERE vs HAVING - Part 1
Rewrite query 4 to only display store id = 1 and total amount > 100
IMPORTANT NOTE: store_id is NOT in the column list so you can NOT put it in the HAVING clause
************************************************************************/

SELECT 
    p.customer_id,
	CONCAT(last_name, ',', first_name) AS customer_name,
    COUNT(payment_id) AS number_of_payment,
    MIN(amount) AS min_payment,
    MAX(amount) AS max_payment,
    ROUND(AVG(amount),2) as avg_payment,
    SUM(amount) as total_amount_paid 
FROM
    payment p JOIN customer c ON p.customer_id = c.customer_id
WHERE store_id = 1 
GROUP BY p.customer_id
HAVING total_amount_paid > 100

/***********************************************************************
Programmer: Khumo Nakedi 
Query 6: WHERE vs HAVING - Part 2
Rewrite query 5 to include store_id in the column list. Now that store_id
is in the column list, you can move the filter to the HAVING clause. 
NOTE: Query 5 is more efficient than Query 6
************************************************************************/

SELECT 
    store_id,
    p.customer_id,
    CONCAT(last_name, ',', first_name) AS customer_name,
    COUNT(payment_id) AS number_of_payment,
    MIN(amount) AS min_payment,
    MAX(amount) AS max_payment,
    ROUND(AVG(amount), 2) AS avg_payment,
    SUM(amount) AS total_amount_paid
FROM
    payment p
        JOIN
    customer c ON p.customer_id = c.customer_id
WHERE
    store_id = 1
GROUP BY store_id,
p.customer_id
HAVING total_amount_paid > 100

